@page "/tables/advanced-table/keyboard"

@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Button
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Button.Enums
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Tables.AdvancedTable.Components
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Tables.AdvancedTable.Components.Columns
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Tables.AdvancedTable.Components.Columns.AdvancedTableSelectColumn
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Tables.AdvancedTable.Components.Footers
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Tables.Shared.Enums

@{
    var cellActivatedPropertyName = nameof(AdvancedTableColumnBase<>.CellActivated);
    var rowClickSelectionPropertyName = nameof(AdvancedTable<>.RowClickSelectionEnabled);
    var itemSelectionAllowedPropertyName = nameof(AdvancedTable<>.ItemSelectionAllowed);
}

<h3>Keyboard navigation</h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    <strong>The whole table body is one Tab stop.</strong> <kbd>Tab</kbd> reaches the table once and lands on the
    <em>focused cell</em> — the cell with the accented outline, and the browser's own focus, so there is never
    a second answer to where the user is. <kbd>Tab</kbd> again leaves for whatever follows the table; <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Tab</kbd> comes back
    to the same cell. Neither the rows, the column headers, nor the controls inside cells are stops of their
    own — the table steps focus over all of them on the way in and out, rather than changing anything about
    the controls a column put in its cells.
</DescriptionBanner>

<DescriptionBanner>
    The <kbd>arrow</kbd> keys move the focused cell one cell at a time and stop at every edge — <kbd>Tab</kbd> is the only way out.
    <kbd>Up</kbd> from the first data row reaches that column's <em>header cell</em>, which is part of the same grid
    rather than a separate set of stops. <strong><kbd>Space</kbd></strong> selects the focused cell's row,
    <strong><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Space</kbd></strong> toggles it and <strong><kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Space</kbd></strong> selects the range from the last
    selected row.
</DescriptionBanner>

<DescriptionBanner>
    <strong><kbd>Enter</kbd> enters a cell</strong>: it raises <code>@cellActivatedPropertyName</code> on the cell's
    column and then moves the focus into the cell's first focusable content, once the render that handler
    caused has landed. <kbd>Tab</kbd> then walks that cell's controls and returns to the cell from the last of them, and
    <strong><kbd>Escape</kbd></strong> returns to it at any point. Clicking a control in a cell enters it too — a cell is
    entered for exactly as long as the focus is inside it — but no amount of <kbd>Tab</kbd> will get you there.
</DescriptionBanner>

<DescriptionBanner>
    The indicator is <code>:focus-visible</code> and nothing else, so the browser decides when it shows: a <kbd>Tab</kbd>
    arrival or an <kbd>arrow</kbd> press paints it, and a mouse click never does. A user who only ever reaches for the
    mouse is shown no keyboard indicator at any point.
</DescriptionBanner>

<h3>Multiple selection</h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    The full gesture set over a table with pinned columns on both edges. The focused cell moves through the
    pinned groups in the order they are shown, and an unpinned cell scrolled behind a pinned one is brought out
    from under it.
</DescriptionBanner>

<DescriptionBanner>
    <strong>This table refuses to select its third row.</strong> That is a rule of this sample and not of the
    table component: <code>@itemSelectionAllowedPropertyName</code> rejects that one row, so no gesture ever
    selects it and its checkbox renders disabled. The keyboard still moves through it like any other row —
    moving and selecting are different things.
</DescriptionBanner>

<DescriptionBanner>
    <strong>The select checkboxes are not Tab stops.</strong> Press <kbd>Enter</kbd> on a select cell to enter it and
    <kbd>Space</kbd> to toggle the checkbox, the same route every other control inside a cell is reached by. The
    select-all checkbox a header can carry has no keyboard route at all — select-all is a mouse action for
    now.
</DescriptionBanner>

<div class="keyboard-container multi">
    <AdvancedTable ItemsProvider="_itemsProvider"
                   SelectionMode="SelectionMode.Multiple"
                   ItemSelectionAllowed="ThirdRowVetoed"
                   ItemIdSelector="item => item.Id"
                   @bind-SelectedItems="_multipleSelectedItems"
                   MaximumHeight="300px">
        <Columns>
            <AdvancedTableSelectColumn PinSide="PinSide.Left" ShowInColumnChooser="false" />
            <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" Width="140">
                <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
            </AdvancedTableColumn>
            <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" Width="155">
                <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
            </AdvancedTableColumn>
            <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Timestamp" Title="Timestamp" Width="220">
                <CellContent>@context.Timestamp</CellContent>
            </AdvancedTableColumn>
            <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Quantity" Title="Quantity" Width="120" PinSide="PinSide.Right">
                <CellContent>@context.Quantity</CellContent>
            </AdvancedTableColumn>
        </Columns>
        <Footer>
            <TableFooter SelectedItemCount="_multipleSelectedItems.Count" />
        </Footer>
    </AdvancedTable>
</div>

<h3>Single selection</h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Under <code>SelectionMode.Single</code> both modifiers are ignored, exactly as they are for a click: every
    <kbd>Space</kbd> variant replaces the selection with the focused cell's row.
</DescriptionBanner>

<DescriptionBanner>
    The two buttons around this table are ordinary page content, there to make the table's own tab order
    visible. <kbd>Tab</kbd> moves from the first button onto one cell of the table and leaves for the second button on
    the next press, however many rows and columns are on screen. The <em>Activating a cell</em> sample below
    puts a real button in every cell and still costs exactly the same two presses.
</DescriptionBanner>

<Button Id="before-single-table" Text="Before the table" Size="ButtonSize.Small" />

<div class="keyboard-container single">
    <AdvancedTable ItemsProvider="_itemsProvider"
                   SelectionMode="SelectionMode.Single"
                   ItemIdSelector="item => item.Id"
                   @bind-SelectedItems="_singleSelectedItems"
                   MaximumHeight="300px">
        <Columns>
            <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Key" Title="Key">
                <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
            </AdvancedTableColumn>
            <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Value" Title="Value">
                <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
            </AdvancedTableColumn>
            <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Quantity" Title="Quantity">
                <CellContent>@context.Quantity</CellContent>
            </AdvancedTableColumn>
        </Columns>
    </AdvancedTable>
</div>

<Button Id="after-single-table" Text="After the table" Size="ButtonSize.Small" />

<h3>The header row</h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    <kbd>Up</kbd> from the first data row reaches the header of that column, and the <kbd>Left</kbd> and <kbd>Right</kbd> arrows walk the
    header row from there. <strong><kbd>Enter</kbd></strong> sorts by the column and <strong><kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd></strong> adds it
    as a further sort level — the position stays in the header row across both, which is what makes building a
    multi-column sort by keyboard possible. <strong><kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Down</kbd></strong> opens the column's filter.
    <strong><kbd>Space</kbd></strong> does nothing on a header: it is a gesture, not a cell to be entered.
</DescriptionBanner>

<DescriptionBanner>
    A sort or a filter is a <em>reshape</em>, and a reshape sends the focused cell back to the first cell of
    the first row — every row has changed place, so the position it named no longer means anything. A position
    in the header row is the exception and survives, because that is where the gestures causing a reshape live.
</DescriptionBanner>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Hiding the column the keyboard is on is the one reshape that does not send it to the first row: it goes to
    the neighboring header instead — the column to the right, or the one to the left when the hidden column
    was the last. Put the keyboard on the <em>Value</em> header and press the button to watch it happen.
</DescriptionBanner>

<Button Id="toggle-value-column"
        Text="@(_valueColumnVisible ? "Hide Value" : "Show Value")"
        Size="ButtonSize.Small"
        OnClick="ToggleValueColumn" />

<div class="keyboard-container header-row">
    <AdvancedTable ItemsProvider="_itemsProvider" RowClickSelectionEnabled="false" MaximumHeight="300px">
        <Columns>
            <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" Sortable="true">
                <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
                <FilterEditor>
                    <StartsWithColumnFilterEditor />
                </FilterEditor>
            </AdvancedTableColumn>
            <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" Sortable="true" Visible="_valueColumnVisible">
                <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
            </AdvancedTableColumn>
            <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Quantity" Title="Quantity" Sortable="true">
                <CellContent>@context.Quantity</CellContent>
            </AdvancedTableColumn>
        </Columns>
    </AdvancedTable>
</div>

<h3>A table with no rows</h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    The no-data placeholder holds the tab stop, so a table stays reachable when it has nothing to navigate.
    <kbd>Arrows</kbd>, <kbd>Space</kbd> and <kbd>Enter</kbd> all do nothing on it and it shows no indicator — but <kbd>Up</kbd> still reaches the header
    row, so sorting and filtering remain available on an empty table.
</DescriptionBanner>

<div class="keyboard-container empty">
    <AdvancedTable ItemsProvider="_emptyItemsProvider" RowClickSelectionEnabled="false" MaximumHeight="300px">
        <Columns>
            <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" Sortable="true">
                <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
            </AdvancedTableColumn>
            <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Value" Title="Value">
                <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
            </AdvancedTableColumn>
        </Columns>
    </AdvancedTable>
</div>

<h3>Activating a cell</h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    <code>@cellActivatedPropertyName</code> is declared per column, so the handler already knows which cell was
    entered. <em>Value</em> wires it and renders a button; <em>Key</em> deliberately does not, so <kbd>Enter</kbd> on a
    <em>Key</em> cell moves nothing and raises nothing — a table where <kbd>Enter</kbd> should act everywhere wires every
    column. <kbd>Enter</kbd> on a <em>Value</em> cell raises the handler and lands the focus on the button, <kbd>Tab</kbd> returns to
    the cell and so does <kbd>Escape</kbd>. Movement and activation do not depend on the selection: this table sets
    <code>@rowClickSelectionPropertyName</code> to <code>false</code>, so <kbd>Space</kbd> selects nothing while the
    <kbd>arrows</kbd> and <kbd>Enter</kbd> keep working.
</DescriptionBanner>

<p>Last activated: @(_lastActivatedKey ?? "nothing yet")</p>

<div class="keyboard-container activation">
    <AdvancedTable ItemsProvider="_itemsProvider"
                   RowClickSelectionEnabled="false"
                   MaximumHeight="300px">
        <Columns>
            <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Key" Title="Key (not wired)">
                <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
            </AdvancedTableColumn>
            <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" CellActivated="@((ExampleTableItem item) => ActivateAsync(item))">
                <CellContent>
                    <Button Text="@context.Value" Size="ButtonSize.Small" OnClick="() => ActivateAsync(context)" />
                </CellContent>
            </AdvancedTableColumn>
        </Columns>
    </AdvancedTable>
</div>

<h3>Virtualized rows</h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Nine hundred rows under <code>TableLoadingMode.Virtualize</code>. Movement costs no round trip at all
    while it stays inside the rendered window, and holding an <kbd>arrow</kbd> travels at the keyboard's own repeat rate.
    Scroll the focused row out of the window with the wheel and press an <kbd>arrow</kbd>: the table scrolls back to where
    the keyboard was left and the focus returns with the row, rather than starting over somewhere else.
</DescriptionBanner>

<div class="keyboard-container virtualized">
    <AdvancedTable ItemsProvider="_manyItemsProvider"
                   Mode="TableLoadingMode.Virtualize"
                   SelectionMode="SelectionMode.Multiple"
                   ItemIdSelector="item => item.Id"
                   @bind-SelectedItems="_virtualizedSelectedItems"
                   MaximumHeight="300px">
        <Columns>
            <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Key" Title="Key">
                <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
            </AdvancedTableColumn>
            <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Value" Title="Value">
                <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
            </AdvancedTableColumn>
            <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Quantity" Title="Quantity">
                <CellContent>@context.Quantity</CellContent>
            </AdvancedTableColumn>
        </Columns>
        <Footer>
            <TableFooter SelectedItemCount="_virtualizedSelectedItems.Count" />
        </Footer>
    </AdvancedTable>
</div>
